WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.452
You have a list of three names: alpha, bravo and charlie.

2
00:00:06.452 --> 00:00:08.559
You want one file for each name.

3
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:16.268
To make several copies of a resource, you need a meta-argument, an argument Terraform itself reads on any resource.

4
00:00:16.268 --> 00:00:18.551
There are two: count, and for_each.

5
00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.071
count takes a number.

6
00:00:20.071 --> 00:00:22.492
Here, the length of the list, so three.

7
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:28.723
It makes three copies, and count.index tells each copy its number: zero, one and two.

8
00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:31.601
Copy zero reads the name in place zero.

9
00:00:31.601 --> 00:00:35.773
So the files say hello alpha, hello bravo, and hello charlie.

10
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.444
But how does Terraform tell these copies apart, from one run to the next?

11
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:41.804
By its address.

12
00:00:41.804 --> 00:00:45.140
The first file is local_file.by_count, zero.

13
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.641
A copy's address is its identity.

14
00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:50.001
With count, the address is a number.

15
00:00:50.001 --> 00:00:53.853
It says where the copy sits in the list, not which name it holds.

16
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:57.771
Now delete alpha from the list, and run plan.

17
00:00:57.771 --> 00:01:00.489
You might expect only one file to go.

18
00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:00.929
Watch.

19
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:04.006
Every name after alpha moves up one place.

20
00:01:04.006 --> 00:01:07.632
Bravo is now in place zero, and charlie in place one.

21
00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:10.991
Terraform compares address by address.

22
00:01:10.991 --> 00:01:12.808
Copy zero said hello alpha.

23
00:01:12.808 --> 00:01:14.692
Now it must say hello bravo.

24
00:01:14.692 --> 00:01:18.124
The content changed, and that forces a replacement.

25
00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:20.260
The same happens to copy one.

26
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.915
It said hello bravo, and now it must say hello charlie.

27
00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:25.986
And copy two is out of range.

28
00:01:25.986 --> 00:01:29.245
There is no place two any more, so it is destroyed.

29
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:32.503
You removed one name, and three files were touched.

30
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:36.312
Put alpha back, and try the same thing with for_each.

31
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.218
for_each takes a map, or a set of strings.

32
00:01:39.218 --> 00:01:42.413
A plain list is rejected, so you wrap it in toset.

33
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:47.300
Each key becomes one copy, and the key is in the address.

34
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.428
local_file.by_each, alpha.

35
00:01:49.428 --> 00:01:49.920
Bravo.

36
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:50.575
Charlie.

37
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.900
Here, the identity is the name, not a position.

38
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:55.697
Delete alpha again.

39
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.488
The count row shifts, just like before.

40
00:01:58.488 --> 00:02:01.076
Two copies replaced, and one destroyed.

41
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.334
In the for_each row, the key alpha is not in the set any more.

42
00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:07.351
So only that copy is destroyed.

43
00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:11.981
Bravo and charlie keep their names, so their addresses did not change.

44
00:02:11.981 --> 00:02:13.873
They stay exactly as they are.

45
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.653
The real plan says: 2 to add, 0 to change, 4 to destroy.

46
00:02:18.653 --> 00:02:21.916
A replace counts as one destroy and one add.

47
00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:26.108
Three of the four destroys come from count, for one removed name.

48
00:02:26.333 --> 00:02:28.166
Files are cheap to replace.

49
00:02:28.166 --> 00:02:31.424
Containers with data, or cloud servers, are not.

50
00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:34.309
The docs give simple advice.

51
00:02:34.309 --> 00:02:38.683
Use count for nearly identical copies, or as an on-off switch.

52
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.619
Use for_each when each copy has its own name or settings.

53
00:02:42.833 --> 00:02:44.598
One limit applies to both.

54
00:02:44.598 --> 00:02:52.063
The plan lists every copy by its address, so Terraform must know the count or the keys before anything exists.

55
00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:56.944
So remember: count copies are known by position, and positions shift.

56
00:02:56.944 --> 00:03:01.283
for_each copies are known by name, so only the removed one goes.
